Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) can be either giants or dwarfs depending on distance; less direct correlation alone.
- Plx (parallax): Larger parallax means closer star; absolute magnitude depends on distance, so important to distinguish giants.
- e_Plx (parallax error): High error reduces reliability of distance and absolute magnitude estimates.
- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature; giants tend to have redder colors (higher B-V), but not definitive alone.
- SpType (spectral type): The luminosity class in spectral type (e.g., III for giants, V for dwarfs) directly indicates if star is giant or not.
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants are intrinsically brighter (lower absolute magnitude values) than dwarfs of similar spectral type.

Step 2.  
10 different conditions for class "0" (star is NOT a giant):  
- SpType is in [types with luminosity class V or IV/V or Vm or V: e.g., B9V, A0IV/V, F6V, G3V, A0Vm, B1V:nn, A0V]  
- Amag (absolute magnitude) is greater than 14.7 (fainter stars are likely dwarfs)  
- B-V is less than 0.5 (bluer stars tend to be dwarfs)  
- Plx is greater than or equal to 2.0 (closer stars with dwarf spectral types)  
- Vmag is greater than 5.0 (generally fainter apparent magnitude for dwarfs at given distance)  
- SpType is in [B9V, A0IV/V, F6V, G3V, A0Vm]  
- Amag is within range of [15, 20] (consistent with dwarf absolute magnitudes in examples)  
- B-V is within range of [-0.05, 0.5] (bluer colors typical of dwarfs in examples)  
- e_Plx is less than 1.1 (more reliable parallax measurements for dwarfs in examples)  
- Plx is within range of [0.05, 15.8] (distance range observed for dwarfs in examples)  

10 different conditions for class "1" (star IS a giant):  
- SpType is in [types with luminosity class III or III/IV or K5/M0III, G8/K0III, K2/K3III, K0III, G8III, K3III, B9III]  
- Amag (absolute magnitude) is less than or equal to 16.3 (giants are brighter, thus lower absolute magnitude)  
- B-V is greater than or equal to 0.9 (redder stars tend to be giants)  
- Plx is less than or equal to 7.5 (giants can be farther, but parallax lower or moderate)  
- Vmag is less than or equal to 8.0 (giants tend to be brighter in apparent magnitude at similar distances)  
- SpType is in [G8/K0III, K2/K3III, K0III, G8III, K3III, K5/M0III, B9III]  
- Amag is within range of [14.7, 16.5] (absolute magnitude range observed for giants)  
- B-V is within range of [0.9, 1.5] (color index range for giants)  
- e_Plx is less than or equal to 1.0 (parallax measurements generally reliable for giants in examples)  
- Plx is within range of [2.3, 7.5] (parallax range observed for giants)

These conditions align well with the provided examples and astrophysical knowledge about giants vs. dwarfs.

