Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Not directly indicative of giant status because it depends on distance; a star can appear bright or dim regardless of size.
- Plx (parallax): Smaller parallax means greater distance; giants tend to be luminous and visible at larger distances, so giants might have smaller parallax values.
- e_Plx (error in parallax): Measurement uncertainty; less relevant to classification but large errors reduce confidence.
- B-V (color index): Indicates star temperature and spectral type; giants often have redder colors (higher B-V), but this alone is not definitive.
- SpType (spectral type): Crucial; giants have spectral types with luminosity class III (e.g., K3III), while dwarfs have V (e.g., G3V).
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Direct measure of intrinsic brightness; giants have brighter absolute magnitudes (lower numerical values) compared to dwarfs of similar spectral type.

Step 2.  
10 different conditions for class "0" (not giant):  
- SpType is in [types ending with V, e.g., G1V, F3V, B9V, A0V, B1V:nn, F6V]  
- Amag >= 15  
- B-V <= 0.5  
- Plx >= 5  
- Vmag >= 7  
- SpType is in [types containing "IV" or "V", e.g., A0IV/V, A0Vm]  
- Amag is within range [15, 20]  
- Plx is within range [3, 20]  
- B-V < 0.2  
- SpType does not contain "III"  

10 different conditions for class "1" (giant):  
- SpType is in [types containing "III", e.g., K3III, B9III, G8III, K0III, K2/K3III, K5/M0III, G8/K0III]  
- Amag < 15  
- B-V >= 0.9  
- Plx < 5  
