Answer:  
Step 1. The relationship between each feature and the task description:

- Vmag (apparent magnitude): Brighter stars (lower Vmag) can be either giants or dwarfs, so Vmag alone is not a strong indicator of giant status.  
- Plx (parallax): Smaller parallax means greater distance. Giants tend to be luminous and visible at larger distances, so giants may have smaller parallaxes compared to dwarfs of similar apparent magnitude.  
- e_Plx (error in parallax): Measurement uncertainty; high error reduces reliability of distance estimate but does not directly indicate giant status.  
- B-V (color index): Giants often have redder colors (higher B-V), especially K and M giants, while dwarfs can have a wide range but often lower B-V for hotter stars.  
- SpType (spectral type): The luminosity class in SpType (e.g., III for giants, V for dwarfs) is a direct indicator of giant or dwarf status.  
- Amag (absolute magnitude): Giants have lower (brighter) absolute magnitudes than dwarfs of the same spectral type, so Amag can help distinguish giants from dwarfs.  

